   149	## 2. The family-2 block and cable structure at `g = 3`
   150	
   151	`(9,6) = (3*3, 2*3)`, so the reduced shape is `(d',n') = (3,2)`, coprime, with
   152	`d' = u = 3` odd and `n' = 2`: **`(9,6)` is the `g = 3` member of family 2**, in
   153	the SHAPE-KILL classification (`shape-kill-uniform-opus5-20260901.md:275`,
   154	`:363`). It is **not** family 3; PB's `3`-periodic block products, its cable
   155	exponent `e(C_2(delta_4^3)) = 36` at `d' = 4`, and its gate `3 | e(iota)` are
   156	`(4,3)`-shape statements and are not transported here. Everything in this
   157	section is re-derived at `(3,2)`.
   158	
   159	**Tubes.** Over `|x| = R` the `d = 9` roots `y_j(x) = sum_{k <= 6} c_k zeta_9^{jk} x^{k/9}`
   160	fall into `d' = 3` blocks `B_i = {j : j = i mod 3}` of size `g = 3`: the leading
   161	terms `c_6 zeta_9^{6j} x^{2/3} = c_6 zeta_3^{2j} x^{2/3}` take exactly three
   162	values, one per residue of `j` mod `3`. The promoted tubular factorisation
   163	(`pi1s4-close-residual-r2-opus5-20260831.md:186-206`) reads
   164	
   165	```text
   166	(2.1)   rho_inf = C_3(delta_3^2) . iota ,   iota in B_{3,1} x B_{3,2} x B_{3,3} ,
   167	(2.2)   e(iota) = (d-1) + 2 delta_aff - n(d-g) = 8 + 2 delta_aff - 36 = 2 delta_aff - 28 ,
   168	```
   169	
   170	with `C_3` blackboard cabling, `e(C_3(delta_3^2)) = g^2 n'(d'-1) = 9*2*2 = 36`,
   171	and `e(rho_inf) = 2 delta_aff + d - 1`. Numerically: `(9,6,2)` gives
   172	`e(iota) = -20`, `(9,6,4)` gives `e(iota) = -16`.
   173	
   174	**The outer datum.** `BP(T) = (Pi_1, Pi_2, Pi_3)` is a fixed point of the Hurwitz
   175	action of `delta_{d'}^{n'} = delta_3^2` and is `n' = 2`-periodic (promoted
   176	`A'(2)`), so it takes at most two values
   177	
   178	```text
   179	X := Pi_1 = Pi_3 ,   Y := Pi_2 ,
   180	```
   181	
   182	and, since `r = d' mod n' = 1`, conjugation by `Pi` is the shift by `-1`:
   183	`Pi X Pi^{-1} = Y`, `Pi Y Pi^{-1} = X`. The total product is the alternating word
   184	`Pi = Pi_1 Pi_2 Pi_3 = X Y X`. Substituting into the first conjugation relation,
   185	`Pi X Pi^{-1} = XYX * X * X^{-1}Y^{-1}X^{-1} = XY X Y^{-1} X^{-1}`, and setting
   186	this equal to `Y` gives, after right-multiplying by `XY`,
   187	
   188	```text
   189	(SK-3.1 at u=3)      X Y X = Y X Y ,
   190	```
   191	
   192	the **braid relation** — SHAPE-KILL's deformed braid relation `X W^m X = W^{m+1}`
   193	at `m = 1`, `W = XY`. The conversion is an identity, not a transplant: at `u = 3`
   194	the two forms coincide.
   195	
   196	**Parity fork.** Each block product `Pi_i` is a product of `g = 3` transpositions,
   197	hence an **odd** permutation: a transposition or a `4`-cycle. This is the exact
   198	point at which family 2 at `g = 3` diverges from family 2 at `g = 2` (where
   199	`X,Y in A_4`) and from family 3 (where `Pi_i in A_4` because `g = 2` there too);
   200	SHAPE-KILL §3.3 item 1 names precisely this divergence. Correspondingly
   201	`sgn(Pi) = (-1)^d = -1`, so `Pi` is a transposition or a `4`-cycle and **`Pi` is
   202	never in `V_4`** — with consequences in §6.
   203	
   204	**The outer solution set, exhaustively.** Solving `XYX = YXY` over the `12` odd
   205	elements of `S_4` gives exactly `60` pairs, in four strata:
   206	
   207	| stratum | description | count | `W = XY` | `Pi = XYX` | `h := Y X^2 Y` |
   208	|---|---|---:|---|---|---|
   209	| `const-T` | `X = Y` a transposition | 6 | `e` | `X` | `e` |
   210	| `const-4c` | `X = Y` a `4`-cycle | 6 | `X^2` | `X^{-1}` | `e` |
   211	| `noncst-T` | `X != Y` transpositions meeting in one letter | 24 | `3`-cycle | transposition | `e` |
   212	| `noncst-4c` | `X != Y` `4`-cycles | 24 | double transposition | transposition | double transposition |
   213	
   214	There are **no mixed solutions** (one transposition and one `4`-cycle). The
   215	promoted `A'(4)` (`Pi^{n'} = Pi^2 in Z(H)`, `H = <Pi_1, Pi>`) holds in all `60`
   216	cases and therefore carries no information at this shape — the `g = 3` analogue
   217	of PB §3's "Route 2 is vacuous" verdict, confirmed here by enumeration rather
   218	than transported. Note also that at `g >= 3` the constant stratum is **not** free
   219	to kill: SHAPE-KILL §3.3 item 3 records that Lemma SK-2 needs the block
   220	`(tau,tau)`, which is not `B_g`-fixed for `g >= 3`. The constant stratum is
   221	therefore carried through §3 and disposed of there on its merits, not by
   222	analogy.
   223	
   224	**The block-level cable action, derived at `d' = 3`.** With the promoted left
   225	Hurwitz convention `sigma_i . (.., t_i, t_{i+1}, ..) = (.., t_i t_{i+1} t_i^{-1}, t_i, ..)`
   226	and `(beta gamma).T = beta.(gamma.T)`, `delta_3 = sigma_1 sigma_2` acts on a
   227	`3`-tuple by
   228	
   229	```text
   230	delta_3 . (u_1,u_2,u_3) = sigma_1 . (u_1, u_2 u_3 u_2^{-1}, u_2) = ((u_1u_2) u_3 (u_1u_2)^{-1}, u_1, u_2) ,
   231	```
   232	
   233	so the conjugator is `u_1 u_2`, i.e. `Pi Pi_{d'}^{-1}` and **not** `Pi`. Cabling
   234	by `C_g` (promoted Lemma 3.3, `C_g(sigma_i).(A,B) = (Pi_A B Pi_A^{-1}, A)`
   235	entrywise) gives, on block tuples with block products `(P_1,P_2,P_3)`,
   236	
   237	```text
   238	(2.4)   C_3(delta_3) . (B_1,B_2,B_3) = ( c_{P_1 P_2}(B_3) , B_1 , B_2 ) ,
   239	```
   240	
   241	`c_h` denoting entrywise conjugation. Internal check: applying block products to
   242	`(2.4)` at `(P_1,P_2,P_3) = (X,Y,X)` sends the block products to
   243	`(XY X (XY)^{-1}, X, Y) = (Y, X, Y)` by the braid relation, and a second
   244	application returns `(X, Y, X)`. So `C_3(delta_3^2)` preserves the block-product
   245	sequence, as it must; a mis-assigned conjugator would break this.
   246	
   247	**The winding computation, and the general exponent identity.** As `x` traverses
   248	one loop, `x^{k/9} -> x^{k/9}zeta_9^{k}`, so `y_j -> y_{j+1}`: `rho_inf` has
   249	permutation the `9`-cycle `j -> j+1` (the one-place-at-infinity condition), and it
   250	cycles the three tubes, so a tube returns to itself after **three** loops. Within
   251	tube `B_i` the mean of the three strands retains only the `3 | k` terms, and the
   252	three deviations are `sum_{3 nmid k} c_k zeta_9^{jk} zeta_3^{rk} x^{k/9}`,
   253	`r = 0,1,2`: at the largest such `k`, namely `k_*`, they are the vertices of an
   254	equilateral triangle, `zeta_3^{k_*}` being a primitive cube root, and that term
   255	dominates all later ones as `R -> infinity`. So for `R` large the tube is a
   256	**rigidly rotating triangle**, turning by `3k_*/9 = k_*/3` full turns over the
   257	three loops. Rotation of a regular `g`-gon by `1/g` turn is
   258	`delta_g := sigma_1 ... sigma_{g-1}`, `e(delta_g) = g-1`, and a full turn is the
   259	full twist `delta_g^g`; hence the accumulated inner braid is `delta_g^{k_*}` and
   260	
   261	```text
   262	(2.3)   k_* = 2d - a - beta_1 ,        e(iota) = k_*(g-1) .
   263	```
   264	
   265	Left half: at `P_inf` the place is `v = s^a`, `u = sum_{k >= d}c_k s^k` with first
   266	non-`a`-divisible exponent `beta_1`; `y = v/u = s^{a-d}U^{-1}`,
   267	`U = sum c_k s^{k-d}`, whose first non-`a`-divisible power `s^{beta_1-d}` survives
   268	inversion; `s ~ x^{-1/d}` then gives `k_* = 2d - a - beta_1`. At `(9,6)`,
   269	`k_* = 15 - beta_1` and `e(iota) = 2k_*`; both halves are checked in §4.
   270	
   271	## 3. THEOREM CABLE-3 — the inner gate at `g = 3`
   272	
   273	SHAPE-KILL §3.3 types `OPEN[SHAPE-2-INNER-g>=3]` and names the missing
   274	ingredient: *the inner braid `iota`, numerically pinned by (1.1) to `e(iota)`
   275	but not otherwise constrained*. At `g = 2` the tube group `B_2 = Z` is abelian
   276	and the exponent sum is a complete invariant, which is what PB §4 exploits. At
   277	`g = 3` the tube group `B_3` is **not** abelian and the exponent sum is **not**
   278	complete — PB §5 ("Where TUBE-2 stops") says so explicitly. The move made here
   279	is different, and it is the reason the `g >= 3` case opens: for a curve with one
   280	place at infinity and a **single** characteristic pair at infinity, `iota` is not
   281	merely constrained by its exponent sum, it is **rigid** — pinned to a rotation by
   282	the Puiseux data, by §2's winding argument. The exponent sum survives only as a
   283	check.
   284	
   285	> **THEOREM CABLE-3 (`PROVED-HERE`, `UNREVIEWED`).** *Let `D` be a residual
   286	> branch curve of family-2 shape `(d,n) = (3g, 2g)`, `g >= 2`, with one place at
   287	> infinity carrying a single characteristic pair `(a; beta_1)`, `a = d - n = g`,
   288	> `gcd(a, beta_1) = 1`; and suppose `phi : pi_1(C^2 - D) ->> S_4` sends every
   289	> meridian to a transposition. Put `k_* := 2d - a - beta_1` and
   290	> `h := Y X^2 Y`, where `(X,Y) = (Pi_1, Pi_2)` is the outer datum of §2. Then the
   291	> block subtuple `T_1` of any `rho_inf`-fixed tuple satisfies*
   292	>
   293	> ```text
   294	> (3.1)     T_1  =  delta_g^{k_*} . c_h(T_1) ,        delta_g = sigma_1 ... sigma_{g-1} ,
   295	> ```
   296	>
   297	> *up to replacing `T_1` by a point of its `B_g`-orbit; and*
   298	>
   299	> ```text
   300	> (3.2)     im(phi)  =  < entries of T_1 ,  entries of c_{XY}(T_1) > .
   301	> ```
   302	
   303	*Proof.* Write `T = (T_1,T_2,T_3)` in blocks and `S := iota . T`, so `S_i = iota_i . T_i`
   304	and each `S_i` has the same block product as `T_i`. Since `(beta gamma).T = beta.(gamma.T)`,
   305	`rho_inf . T = T` reads `C_3(delta_3^2) . S = T`. Applying `(2.4)` twice, with block
   306	products `(X,Y,X)` before the first application and `(Y,X,Y)` before the second,
   307	
   308	```text
   309	C_3(delta_3^2) . S = ( c_{YX}(S_2) ,  c_{XY}(S_3) ,  S_1 )  =  (T_1, T_2, T_3) .
   310	```
   311	
   312	Conjugation commutes with the Hurwitz action (the action is by words in the
   313	entries), so composing the three relations around the cycle
   314	`T_1 <- T_2 <- T_3 <- T_1` gives
   315	
   316	```text
   317	T_3 = iota_1 . T_1 ,
   318	T_2 = c_{XY}(iota_3 . T_3) = (iota_3 iota_1) . c_{XY}(T_1) ,
   319	T_1 = c_{YX}(iota_2 . T_2) = (iota_2 iota_3 iota_1) . c_{(YX)(XY)}(T_1) ,
   320	```
   321	
   322	that is `T_1 = beta . c_h(T_1)` with `beta := iota_2 iota_3 iota_1 in B_g` and
   323	`h = (YX)(XY) = Y X^2 Y`. **Only the ordered product `beta` survives; the
   324	distribution of `iota` among the three tubes is irrelevant.** Its exponent sum is
   325	`e(beta) = e(iota)`.
   326	
   327	By §2's winding computation, over the three loops of `x` that return a tube to
   328	itself the `g` strands of that tube move as a rigidly rotating regular `g`-gon
   329	through `k_*/g` full turns; blackboard cabling adds no twist. The accumulated
   330	inner braid is therefore `delta_g^{k_*}`, and `beta` — which is exactly that
   331	accumulated braid, read from whichever tube carries the basepoint and under
   332	whichever identification of the tube's strands with `{1,..,g}` — is **conjugate
   333	in `B_g`** to `delta_g^{k_*}`. Write `beta = gamma delta_g^{k_*} gamma^{-1}`.
   334	Since `c_h` commutes with the Hurwitz action,
   335	
   336	```text
   337	T_1 = gamma delta_g^{k_*} gamma^{-1} . c_h(T_1)
   338	   <=>  (gamma^{-1}.T_1) = delta_g^{k_*} . c_h(gamma^{-1}.T_1) ,
   339	```
   340	
   341	so replacing `T_1` by `gamma^{-1}.T_1` — which changes neither the block product,
   342	nor the subgroup generated by the entries, nor therefore `(3.2)` — puts the
   343	condition in the form `(3.1)`.
   344	
   345	For `(3.2)`: `T_3 = iota_1 . T_1` and `T_2 = (iota_3 iota_1).c_{XY}(T_1)` lie in
   346	the `B_g`-orbits of `T_1` and of `c_{XY}(T_1)`, and the Hurwitz action preserves
   347	the subgroup generated by the entries of a tuple. Hence the nine entries of `T`
   348	generate `< T_1 > . < c_{XY}(T_1) >` as claimed. `[]`
   349	
   350	**Consistency at `g = 2`.** There `delta_2 = sigma`, `e(iota) = k_*`, and on the
   351	promoted `(6,4)` row `X,Y` are distinct `3`-cycles (SK-4) with `h = YX^2Y = X` —
   352	hand-evaluated, e.g. `(234)(124)^2(234) = (234)`. Since `sigma^2 = c_{uv} = c_X`
   353	on a block `(u,v)` with `uv = X`, `(3.1)` becomes `T_1 = sigma^{k_*+2}.T_1`,
   354	solvable exactly when `3 | k_*+2` — verbatim PB §5's Control 2 gate. CABLE-3
   355	**specialises to the promoted `g = 2` statement**; it is not a new convention,
   356	and §4 Control 2 runs both halves of it.
   357	
   358	**Solution at `g = 3`.** The tuple `T_1 = (t_1,t_2,t_3)` runs over triples of
   359	transpositions with product `X`; there are `1080` pairs (outer solution, `T_1`)
   360	in total. `delta_3^3` is the full twist and acts as `c_X`, so the fixed set of
   361	`delta_3^{k}.c_h` depends only on `k` modulo `3 * ord(X) in {6,12}`. Exhaustive
   362	evaluation over all `60` outer solutions and all `1080` subtuples, for every
   363	residue of `k`, gives the following table; entries are (number of fixed `T_1`
   364	with `im(phi) = S_4`) / (number of fixed `T_1`).
   365	
   366	```text
   367	   k   |  const-4c   const-T    noncst-4c   noncst-T
   368	  -----+---------------------------------------------
   369	   0   |    96/96      0/120        0/0      264/480
   370	  -1   |     0/0        0/6         0/0        0/24
   371	  -2   |     0/0        0/30       96/96      48/120
   372	  -3   |     0/0        0/24        0/0       72/96
   373	  -4   |    24/24       0/30        0/0       48/120
   374	  -5   |     0/0        0/6         0/0        0/24
   375	  -6   |     0/0        0/120     384/384    264/480
   376	```
   377	
   378	(the table is `12`-periodic in `k`; `k = -7,...,-12` repeat `k = -1,...,-6`).
   379	Reading it:
   380	
   381	* **`const-T` is dead at every `k`.** Here `W = XY = X^2 = e`, so by `(3.2)`
   382	  `im(phi) = <T_1>`, and `T_1` is three transpositions with product a
   383	  transposition, which generates a subgroup of order at most `6`. This is the
   384	  `g = 3` replacement for Lemma SK-2, proved on its merits rather than assumed.
   385	* **`const-4c` requires `4 | k_*`;** **`noncst-4c` requires `k_* = 2 (mod 4)`;**
   386	  **`noncst-T` requires `k_*` even, given `3 nmid k_*`.**
   387	* `3 nmid k_*` is automatic: it is the definition of `k_*` together with
   388	  `a = g = 3`, and it is equivalent to the permutation of `rho_inf` being a
   389	  `9`-cycle, i.e. to one place at infinity.
   390	
   391	Combining the three live strata:
   392	
   393	> **GATE-3.** *On a family-2 row with `d' = 3`, `g = 3` (i.e. `(d,n) = (9,6)`),
   394	> one place at infinity and a single characteristic pair, a `rho_inf`-fixed tuple
   395	> with `im(phi) = S_4` exists only if*
   396	>
   397	> ```text
   398	> (3.3)     k_*  is EVEN   <=>   4 | e(iota)   <=>   2 | delta_aff   <=>   2 | c .
   399	> ```
   400	>
   401	> *Moreover the stratum is then pinned: `k_* = 2 (mod 4)` allows only `noncst-4c`
   402	> and `noncst-T`; `k_* = 0 (mod 4)` allows only `const-4c` and `noncst-T`.*
   403	
   404	The equivalences in `(3.3)` are `k_* = 15 - beta_1`, `delta_aff = 29 - beta_1`
   405	(so `delta_aff - k_* = 14` is even), `beta_1 = 27 - c` (so `k_* = c - 12`), and
   406	`e(iota) = 2k_*`.
   407	
   408	**The exponent sum alone would give nothing.** For each of the `1080` pairs
   409	(outer solution, `T_1`) the twisted stabiliser `{beta in B_3 : beta.c_h(T_1) = T_1}`
   410	is a coset of `Stab(T_1)`, and its exponent-sum image was computed modulo
   411	`24` (a period, since `delta_3^{3 ord(X)}` acts trivially and
   412	`6 ord(X) | 24`). At `e(beta) = -20` and at `e(beta) = -16` alike, **all `1080`
   413	pairs admit some `beta` of the required exponent sum**, `744` of them with full
   414	image. So the `g = 2` argument shape — pin `iota` by `e(iota)` and read off a
   415	stabiliser index — is *provably vacuous* at `g = 3`, exactly as SHAPE-KILL §3.3
   416	predicted. Every bit of GATE-3 comes from rigidity, not from the exponent sum;
   417	the exponent sum survives only as the consistency check `e(delta_3^{k_*}) = 2k_*
   418	= e(iota)` of §4.
   419	
   420	**GATE-3 is not vacuous.** The residues `k_* = 1, 5, 7, 11 (mod 12)` — that is,
   421	`gcd(k_*, 6) = 1` — admit **no** fixed tuple with full image, in any stratum.
   422	Half the admissible residues die. This is the exact `g = 3` analogue of the
   423	`g = 2` gate `3 | e(iota)+2`, derived from scratch, and it has a different
   424	modulus and a different arithmetic content, as the charge anticipated.
